<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarjunakonda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagarjunakonda is a historic Buddhist site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, named after the Buddhist philosopher Nagarjuna. In the 3rd and 4th centuries CE the valley held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a>, capital of the Ikshvaku dynasty, whose royal women endowed many stupas, monasteries and shrines that drew monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and beyond. Most of the valley now lies under the Nagarjuna Sagar reservoir, and its rescued monuments stand on an island in the lake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda.jpg" alt="Nagarjunakonda" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sabyk2001 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddhist_site_3rd_century_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
